-- Q11170: Agency and Regulation [hard/L4]
-- Issues: #3
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Under the Restatement (Second) of Torts Section 552, CPAs are liable to foreseen users who rely on negligent audit work. Pacific Investors was specifically identified to Graham, qualifying as a foreseen user entitled to recover. Under the Ultramares doctrine (Ultramares Corp. v. Touche, 1931), liability extends only to parties in privity or near privity, so Pacific Investors cannot recover for ordinary negligence.
Wrong (B): Under Ultramares, even an identified user like Pacific Investors cannot recover for negligence without privity, so stating both approaches allow recovery for Pacific is incorrect.
Wrong (C): The Restatement does not extend liability to all relying parties; it is limited to foreseen users or classes of users, not anyone who relies.
Wrong (D): This reverses the two doctrines. Ultramares is the more restrictive standard (near privity), while the Restatement is broader (foreseen users).$EXPL$
WHERE id = 11170;

-- Q11171: Agency and Regulation [hard/L3]
-- Issues: #3
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): Under the Securities Exchange Act of 1934, Section 18 imposes liability for false statements in SEC filings and allows defendants a good-faith defense. Rule 10b-5 is the broader antifraud provision requiring plaintiffs to prove scienter (intent to deceive or reckless disregard of truth).
Wrong (A): This reverses the relationship. Rule 10b-5 requires proof of scienter from the plaintiff, while Section 18 does not require scienter but allows a good-faith defense.
Wrong (B): Rule 10b-5 does not provide a due diligence defense. Section 18 offers a good-faith defense, which is distinct from due diligence under Section 11 of the 1933 Act.
Wrong (C): Section 18 applies to written documents filed with the SEC, not oral statements. Rule 10b-5 covers fraud in connection with any securities transaction.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 11171;

-- Q11172: Agency and Regulation [medium/L3]
-- Issues: #3
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Under Securities Act of 1933 Section 5(b)(1), during the waiting period between filing and effectiveness of a registration statement, the underwriter may distribute a preliminary prospectus (red herring) to gauge investor interest. Oral offers are also permitted, but binding sales are prohibited.
Wrong (A): No sales of securities may occur during the waiting period. Selling shares, even to qualified institutional buyers, is prohibited until the registration becomes effective.
Wrong (C): Binding purchase commitments constitute sales, which are prohibited during the waiting period regardless of the investor''s accredited status.
Wrong (D): The final prospectus may only be delivered and shares sold after the registration statement becomes effective, not during the waiting period.$EXPL$
