-- Q11174: Agency and Regulation [hard/L4]
-- Issues: #3
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Under the predominant purpose test, a mixed goods-and-services contract is governed entirely by whichever body of law corresponds to the dominant element. Services account for $85,000 (71%) of the $120,000 price, so common law governs. Canton must prove a material breach under the substantial performance doctrine to justify rejection.
Wrong (B): UCC Article 2 does not govern because services predominate. The perfect tender rule under Section 2-601 applies only when the UCC governs a goods contract.
Wrong (C): Courts do not split a single contract between common law and UCC. The predominant purpose test applies one body of law to the entire agreement.
Wrong (D): Manufactured fixtures are not automatically classified as goods. The predominant purpose test considers the overall contract, not just the physical items involved.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 11174;

-- Q11175: Agency and Regulation [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #3
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): Under Section 3(b) of the Securities Act of 1933 and SEC Regulation A (Rule 251 et seq.), a simplified registration process allows securities offerings of up to $75 million with reduced disclosure requirements. This "mini-registration" permits public solicitation while avoiding full S-1 registration costs.
Wrong (A): Rule 506(b) of Regulation D is a private placement exemption that prohibits general solicitation and does not offer a simplified registration process for public offerings.
Wrong (B): Rule 147 is the intrastate offering exemption, requiring all sales to be made within a single state. It does not provide a simplified registration for $50 million national offerings.
Wrong (C): Section 3(a)(11) exempts certain bank-issued securities, not startup equity offerings seeking public capital.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 11175;

-- Q11176: Agency and Regulation [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #3
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Under Section 14 of the Securities Exchange Act of 1934, any person soliciting proxies must furnish shareholders with a proxy statement containing material information about the matters subject to a vote, including director nominees, executive compensation, and proposals such as mergers.
Wrong (A): While governance documents may be relevant, the 1934 Act specifically requires a proxy statement with material disclosures, not copies of articles of incorporation or bylaws.
Wrong (C): The SEC does not require companies to summarize enforcement actions in proxy solicitation materials. The proxy statement focuses on matters being voted upon.
Wrong (D): A binding commitment letter guaranteeing vote outcomes does not exist in securities law. Proxy solicitation materials inform shareholders, not guarantee results.$EXPL$
