-- Q11177: Agency and Regulation [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #3
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Under Section 10(b) of the Securities Exchange Act of 1934 and SEC Rule 10b-5, it is unlawful for any person to use material nonpublic information to trade securities. Burke''s purchase of Meridian shares based on knowledge of the unannounced acquisition constitutes insider trading.
Wrong (B): Section 11 of the Securities Act of 1933 imposes liability for material misstatements in registration statements filed during public offerings, not for insider trading.
Wrong (C): Section 18 covers liability for false statements in documents filed with the SEC, not for trading on nonpublic information.
Wrong (D): Section 16(a) requires insiders to report transactions but does not itself prohibit trading. The substantive prohibition against insider trading arises under Section 10(b) and Rule 10b-5.$EXPL$
