  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Under Section 3(a)(11) of the Securities Act of 1933 and SEC Rule 147, the intrastate offering exemption requires securities to be offered and sold exclusively to residents of a single state. If even one sale is made to an out-of-state investor, the entire exemption is destroyed and the full offering may require registration.
Wrong (A): Regulation D Rule 506(b) is a private placement exemption with its own requirements. The intrastate offering described here falls under Rule 147, not Regulation D.
Wrong (C): Section 3(a)(11) does not permit out-of-state sales after the offering closes. Any sale to a non-resident, regardless of timing, destroys the exemption.
