-- Q11188: Agency and Regulation [easy/L1]
-- Issues: #3
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Under the Restatement (Third) of Agency Section 3.07, an agency relationship terminates by operation of law when the subject matter becomes illegal or is destroyed, making performance impossible. Illegality extinguishes the agent''s authority without any action by the parties.
Wrong (A): A request for a higher commission is a negotiation matter that does not automatically terminate the agency. The relationship continues unless the parties agree otherwise.
Wrong (C): Hiring additional agents for the same task does not terminate the existing agency. Multiple agents can serve the same principal concurrently unless the agreement prohibits it.
Wrong (D): An agent''s vacation without notice may constitute a breach of duty but does not terminate the agency relationship by operation of law.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 11188;

-- Q11189: Agency and Regulation [easy/L1]
-- Issues: #3
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Under Rule 10b-5 of the Securities Exchange Act of 1934, scienter means the defendant acted with intent to deceive or manipulate, or with reckless disregard for the truth. This mental-state requirement distinguishes Rule 10b-5 from Section 11 of the Securities Act of 1933, which does not require proof of intent.
Wrong (B): Good faith is a defense relevant under Section 18 of the 1934 Act, not the standard of culpability that scienter represents. Scienter is the opposite of good faith.
Wrong (C): Physical harm is not an element of a Rule 10b-5 claim. The relevant injury is financial loss from securities fraud, not physical damage.
Wrong (D): Scienter requires awareness and intent, not unawareness. A defendant who was truly unaware of the transaction would lack the mental state needed for liability.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 11189;

-- Q11191: Business Law: Contracts [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #3
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Under the common law pre-existing duty rule (Restatement (Second) of Contracts Section 73), a promise to pay more for work a party is already contractually obligated to perform is unenforceable for lack of new consideration. Rivera was already bound to complete the rewiring for $8,000 and offered nothing additional in exchange for the extra $2,000.
Wrong (A): Voluntary agreement alone does not make a modification enforceable under common law. A bargained-for exchange of new consideration is required.
Wrong (C): Increased difficulty of performing an existing obligation does not constitute new consideration. The pre-existing duty rule bars enforcement absent genuinely new performance.
Wrong (D): Enforceability depends on whether new consideration was provided, not on whether the original contract contained a no-modification clause.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 11191;

-- Q11192: Business Law: Contracts [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #3
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Under UCC Section 2-509, in a shipment contract (FOB shipping point), risk of loss passes to the buyer when the seller delivers the goods to the carrier. Chandler fulfilled its obligation upon delivering the tablets to the carrier, so Drake bears the loss.
Wrong (B): Under FOB shipping point, the seller''s obligation ends at the point of shipment. The fact that goods never reached the destination does not keep risk with the seller.
Wrong (C): The carrier is a bailee, not the risk-bearer. Under UCC Section 2-509, risk transfers to the buyer at shipment, not upon delivery to the buyer.
Wrong (D): Risk transfer under FOB shipping point is not contingent on payment. Risk passes upon delivery to the carrier regardless of whether the buyer has paid.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 11192;

-- Q11193: Business Law: Contracts [hard/L4]
-- Issues: #3
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): Under UCC Section 2-302 and the Restatement (Second) of Contracts Section 208, a court may refuse to enforce a contract that is unconscionable. Procedural unconscionability exists here (language barrier, high-pressure tactics, lack of meaningful choice), and substantive unconscionability exists because the price is grossly inflated ($50,000 for $3,000 of work).
Wrong (A): A voluntary signature does not validate an unconscionable contract. Courts examine the fairness of the bargaining process and terms, not merely whether the party signed.
Wrong (B): Procedural unconscionability alone may be insufficient. Most courts require a showing of both procedural and substantive unconscionability to refuse enforcement.
Wrong (C): Substantive unconscionability alone may also be insufficient. Courts typically require both elements to be present before declaring a contract unenforceable.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 11193;

-- Q11198: Business Law: Contracts [medium/L3]
-- Issues: #3
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Under the Restatement (Second) of Contracts Section 175, a contract is voidable if a party''s assent was induced by an improper threat that left no reasonable alternative. Weston''s threat to spread false information constitutes economic duress because it goes beyond legitimate business pressure and left Patterson no reasonable choice.
Wrong (B): Fraud requires a material misrepresentation of fact under Restatement Section 164. Weston threatened future harmful action rather than making a false statement about present facts.
Wrong (C): Undue influence requires a relationship of trust or dominance, such as a fiduciary relationship. A standard vendor-customer relationship does not qualify.
Wrong (D): Mutual mistake requires both parties to share an erroneous assumption about an existing fact. Here, the price reduction was coerced, not based on a shared misunderstanding.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 11198;

-- Q11199: Business Law: Contracts [medium/L3]
-- Issues: #3
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (C): Under the Restatement (Second) of Contracts Section 280, a novation occurs when all original parties agree to substitute a new party for an existing one, completely releasing the original party from liability. The three elements are a valid existing obligation, agreement by all parties, and full discharge of the original obligor.
Wrong (A): An assignment transfers contractual rights (such as the right to receive payment), not the substitution of an entire party with a release from liability.
Wrong (B): A delegation transfers duties to a third party, but the original party remains secondarily liable under Restatement Section 318. Franklin was fully released here.
Wrong (D): An accord and satisfaction involves a new agreement to accept different performance in satisfaction of the original obligation, not the substitution of a new party.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 11199;

-- Q11200: Business Law: Contracts [easy/L2]
-- Issues: #3
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Under the Restatement (Second) of Contracts Section 39(1), a counteroffer operates as a rejection of the original offer and simultaneously constitutes a new offer. Klein''s $16,000 proposal extinguished Hoffman''s original $18,000 offer, terminating Klein''s power of acceptance.
Wrong (A): Klein cannot accept the original terms because the counteroffer terminated that offer by operation of law. Timeliness is irrelevant once the offer has been extinguished.
Wrong (C): Formal revocation is unnecessary because the counteroffer itself destroyed the original offer. Hoffman did not need to take any additional action.
Wrong (D): The offer was terminated by the counteroffer, not by a revocation. Whether Hoffman communicated a revocation is irrelevant to the analysis.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 11200;
